# Release Notes Process

`RELEASE_NOTES.md` is a cumulative changelog that carries historical release notes for all versions (newest first).

Notes are written **as work lands**, not at tag time: every user-facing change adds its bullets to an `## Unreleased` section at the top of the file. At release time — when the version number is bumped — that heading is renamed to the real `## vX.Y.Z`.

## Format

```markdown
# Release Notes

<!-- Rename this "## Unreleased" heading to the real "## vX.Y.Z" at release
     time — publish.yml's notes extractor matches ^## v${TAG}$ exactly, so an
     "Unreleased" heading is not picked up for the GitHub release. -->
## Unreleased

- **Feature Name** -- One-sentence description of the feature and its user-facing value.
- **Another Feature** -- Description.

## vX.Y.Z (last release)

- ...

## vX.Y.W (previous release)

- ...
```

## Rules

1. Add bullets for user-facing changes under `## Unreleased` at the top of the file (below the `# Release Notes` heading), as the work merges. If no `## Unreleased` section exists, create one — together with the HTML comment reminder shown above.
2. There is at most ONE `## Unreleased` section, and it is always the topmost section. Never create a second one; append to the existing one.
3. Never write a `## vX.Y.Z` heading ahead of the actual release — the version number is not known until the bump, and a premature heading makes the publish workflow attach notes to the wrong tag.
4. Each bullet must start with a bold feature name followed by a concise description.
5. Focus on user-facing changes: new capabilities, changed behavior, removed functionality.
6. Do NOT list internal refactors, CI changes, or dependency bumps unless they affect users.
7. For patch releases (x.y.Z), list bug fixes instead of features.
8. NEVER remove or edit older version sections -- the file is the historical record.
9. The publish workflow extracts the section matching the tagged version and includes it on the GitHub Release page. Its matcher is exact (`^## v${TAG}$`) and stops at the next `^## v` heading, so an `## Unreleased` heading yields empty release notes.

## Workflow

### As features merge to main

1. Add bullets under the `## Unreleased` heading at the top of the file (creating the section if it isn't there).
2. Keep bullets in the section ordered most-significant first; a later change may be inserted above earlier ones.

### At release time (version bump)

1. Decide the version from the accumulated `## Unreleased` bullets (features -> minor, fixes only -> patch, breaking -> major).
2. Bump the version in `Cargo.toml` and refresh `Cargo.lock`.
3. Rename `## Unreleased` to `## vX.Y.Z` — matching the bumped `Cargo.toml` version exactly — and delete the HTML comment reminder that sat above it.
4. Review and finalize the section's wording, then commit as `chore(release): X.Y.Z` (version bump + renamed section together).
5. Run `git tag vX.Y.Z` and push the tag -- CI extracts the matching section and publishes the release with those notes.
6. The next user-facing change starts a fresh `## Unreleased` section (with the comment) at the top.
